# calmoji v@VERSION@ — calendar files

Ready-made `.ics` calendar layers from
[calmoji](https://github.com/propertools/Calmoji-Forge), for **@YEARS@**,
in two alignments:

* `academic`: each year runs September → August
* `calendar`: each year runs January → December

Pick one alignment and stick with it.

Every time in these files is in **UTC**, and your calendar app shows it in
your local time. That's why the calendar names don't say "UTC".

---

## What's in each year's folder

```
<year>/<alignment>/
├── seasons_<year>.ics            ← 🌗 Seasons (all-day markers)
├── emoji_clock_<year>.ics        ← 🧿 Emoji Clock, every day
├── focus/
│   └── focus_<YYYY-MM>.ics       ← 🧠 Focus — Open, one file per month
└── meetings/
    └── meetings_<YYYY-MM>.ics    ← 🕒 Meetings — Open, one file per month
```

Each file names its calendar for you: the calendar name inside is exactly
the name above (🌗 Seasons, 🧠 Focus — Open, 🕒 Meetings — Open, 🧿 Emoji
Clock), so apps that create a calendar on import get it right.

Months are in **UTC**. If you're west of UTC, an event late in the evening
on the last day of a month may be in the next month's file.

Every file is kept under @MAX_EVENTS@ events and @MAX_KIB@ KB, so it fits the
import limits reported for Google, Outlook and Proton. This bundle covers
@YEARS@, and stops at 2036 because Proton accepts events only up to 2037.

---

## Importing

Import each layer into **its own calendar** (for example "🧠 Focus — Open",
"🕒 Meetings — Open"), so you can colour it and switch it on and
off on its own. The calmoji
[playbook](https://github.com/propertools/Calmoji-Forge/blob/main/docs/PLAYBOOK.md)
explains how to use the layers day to day.

* **Apple Calendar:** File → Import → choose the file → choose the
  calendar.
* **Google Calendar:** Settings → Import & export → choose the file and
  the destination calendar.
* **Outlook, Fastmail, Thunderbird and others:** use your app's "Import"
  option.

**Start with this month and next.** Focus blocks and meeting slots come one
file per month; add more months as you go. It's a gentler start anyway, and
each event is in exactly one file, so nothing is imported twice.

To refresh a layer later, delete that calendar, recreate it, and import
again. Your own events, in your own calendars, are untouched.

### Upgrading from v0.1.0

Delete the old calmoji calendars and import fresh. Event times and
identifiers changed (see the
[changelog](https://github.com/propertools/Calmoji-Forge/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md)),
so importing over the old ones would leave you with duplicates.

---

## Good to know in v@VERSION@

* **Meeting slots are fixed in UTC all year.** Your calendar app shows
  them in your local time. In cities with daylight saving time (Brussels,
  Havana, Seattle) a slot's local time is an hour earlier in winter than in
  summer. Each slot's description says exactly when it falls locally.
* **The 🧿 Emoji Clock (EBI48) appears every day**, one event per half-hour
  that repeats daily through the year.
* **Months are in UTC**, as above.

More is planned for v0.2. See the
[roadmap](https://github.com/propertools/Calmoji-Forge#-roadmap).

---

## Verifying the files

`MANIFEST.sha256` lists the SHA-256 hash of every `.ics` file. From
inside this folder:

```bash
shasum -a 256 -c MANIFEST.sha256
```

(On Linux, `sha256sum -c MANIFEST.sha256` works too.) Every line should
end in `OK`.

You can also regenerate any year yourself and compare. The output is
byte-identical (calmoji also leaves a small `.calmoji-output` marker in the
folders it fills, which isn't part of this release, hence the `--exclude`):

```bash
git clone https://github.com/propertools/Calmoji-Forge.git
cd Calmoji-Forge
git checkout v@VERSION@
python3 calmoji.py --year=2027 --calendar-alignment=academic --output-dir=check
diff -r --exclude=.calmoji-output check /path/to/this/folder/2027/academic
```

